  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "The short answer",
      "items": [
        "Live UI updates use an in-process event bus pushed over SSE and are best-effort per instance",
        "Workflow email goes through a leased, retried database outbox with at-least-once delivery",
        "Non-workflow notifications are sent inline and recorded in-app"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "slate",
      "title": "Not shown here",
      "items": [
        "The SSE route and the browser at the end of the stream, the Bucket workflow, which publishes to the same bus, outbox and callback queue as Ticket services",
        "the mail and chat providers (see the integrations diagram)",
        "and the schedulers that trigger retries"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "violet",
      "title": "Trade-offs",
      "items": [
        "The event bus, the proxy and route rate limiters, and the in-process job lock are per-instance: they are plain memory on globalThis; on several instances a user may miss a live update and a rate limit is enforced per instance, while Postgres advisory locks and leases do stop duplicate sweeps",
        "Workflow email is delivered at least once, so a timeout after the provider accepted a message can send a duplicate: a lease plus backoff is used instead of a transactional hand-off to the provider"
      ]
    }
  ]
